'''triplasian†''' (trī-plā′s̮i-a̤n), ''a.'' [< Gr. τριπλάσιος, three times as many, < τρεῖς (τρι-), three, + -πλάσιος as in διπλάσιος, twofold.] Threefold; triple; treble.

'''triplasic''' (trī-plas′ik), ''a.'' [< LL. ''triplasius'', < Gr. τριπλάσιος: see ''triplasian''.] Triple; threefold; specifically, in ''anc. pros.'', constituting the proportion of three to one: as, the ''triplasic'' ratio (of times or semeia in thesis and arsis); characterized by such a proportion of thesis and arsis: as, a ''triplasic'' foot. {{smaller|The only clear instance of a triplasic foot seems to be an amphibrach standing at the beginning of a colon or verse of Ionics a majore.}}

